 "reciprocal_saturation": {
  "rule": "RECIP_SAT: for finite positive d with bits(d) > 0x7EF311C7 (d > 1.6158e38) the reciprocal seed is +0, so the three Newton steps return +0 (true 1/d < 2^-127). Implemented in tools/hdc_golden.py reciprocal() and the RTL reciprocal pipelines (rtl/hdc/ot_hdc_sfu.sv, rtl/hdc/ot_hdc_sfu_q.sv, reference rtl/test/ot_hdc_sfu_ref.sv) at 27d30c15.",
  "why_it_matters": "silu(g) = g * reciprocal(1 + exp(-g)) reaches the saturated range for every gate g <= -87.98; full Qwen3-8B produces 1.8-4.1 million such SiLU elements per evaluation run here (modes.*.recip_sat_hits, about 1.4e-5 of gate elements). Before 27d30c15 the golden and RTL returned NaN for them.",
  "emulation": "The emulation applies the same rule (qwen3_deployment_quality.reciprocal_g); the results were computed with it before 27d30c15 landed and are unchanged by it.",
  "verification": "After 27d30c15: the GPU contract forward is bit-exact with tools/hdc_golden.py@27d30c15 in every logit of the reduced Qwen3 vehicle at groups 1, 4, 64, 1,024 and 8,192, BF16 and FP8 KV (20 positions; blocked prefill and batched decode), and reciprocal/silu agree bit-exactly on the saturation boundary, 3.4e38 and g in [-200, 90] (tests/test_qwen3_deployment_quality.py). Numerics identical, so no full-model rerun was needed."
 }
